<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasily III of Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1479">1479</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasily III was grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1505 to 1533, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> and father of Ivan the Terrible. He completed the gathering of the Russian lands, ending the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> in 1510, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1514 and annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> in 1521. He also abolished the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a> in 1523.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow.jpg" alt="Vasily III of Moscow" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: André Thévet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasili_III_of_Russia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vasily was born on 25 March 1479, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> and his second wife Sophia Paleologue. Ivan's grandson Dmitry Ivanovich became heir presumptive in 1490 and co-ruler in 1498. In 1499 Vasily was forgiven for a conspiracy against Dmitry and received the title of grand prince of Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>. In April 1502 Ivan III had Dmitry and his mother Elena of Moldavia arrested and, three days later, named Vasily his successor. Vasily married Solomonia Saburova on 4 September 1505, after a bride-show of some 1,500 noble girls, a custom described by the diplomat Sigismund von Herberstein.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Vasily believed that nothing should limit the power of the grand prince, and he had the active support of the church against the boyar opposition. Those who criticised his policies were exiled or executed, among them the diplomat Ivan Bersen-Beklemishev in 1525. He continued his father's gathering of the Russian lands. In 1510 he had the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> republic arrested, and the veche bell was removed and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>; 300 of the city's most noble families were resettled on Moscow lands. In 1521 he took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> after imprisoning its prince, Ivan V Ivanovich, and he annexed the Starodub principality in 1523.</p><p>In the Lithuanian–Muscovite War he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> three times, with artillery and engineers supplied by the rebel Lithuanian prince Michael Glinski. The city surrendered on 31 July 1514, and a treaty of 1522 left it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In 1521 the Crimean khan Mehmed I Giray reached the walls of Moscow and forced him to accept a treaty making him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Later campaigns brought Russian influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, and in 1523 he founded Vasilsursk. Stone churches and fortifications were built, including the Cathedral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> in the Kremlin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After more than twenty years of marriage with no heir, Vasily divorced Solomonia, falsely accusing her of witchcraft, and in 1526 married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elena-glinskaya">Elena Glinskaya</a>. She bore a son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>, and later a second, Yuri. Vasily died on 3 December 1533, after an abscess on his hip that two German doctors could not cure. He took the monastic name Varlaam before he died. He did not officially take the title of tsar, but in a treaty of 1514 with Emperor Maximilian I he was called kaiser, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> later cited.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasili III of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vasily III</b> captured Smolensk in 1514, although a Polish-Lithuanian army defeated his forces at the Battle of Orsha the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tsardom of Russia grew out of the Grand Duchy of Moscow, which under Ivan III (r. 1462–1505) and <b>Vasily III</b> (r. 1505–1533) had absorbed Novgorod, Tver, Pskov and Ryazan, thrown off the overlordship of the Great Horde and become the dominant power among the Russian principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards Moscow's influence grew steadily, and in 1521 <b>Vasily III</b> arrested Ivan Ivanovich, the last grand prince, and annexed Ryazan.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Grand Principality of Ryazan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vasily III</b> arrested Shemyachich in 1523 and abolished the principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-novgorod-seversky">Principality of Novgorod-Seversky</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1510 <b>Vasily III</b> ended its independence, removed the veche bell and deported hundreds of leading families.</blockquote><a href="/state/pskov-republic">Pskov Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vasily III of Moscow"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 5342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
